// Rewrite all 22 Principle descriptions to app/database/ontology/writing-style.md
// Generated 2026-10-08. Sets description_short and description_full only;
// handle and name stay as written. update_principle has no side effects to reproduce.
UNWIND [
  {handle: 'principle:audience-determines-governing-duty', short: 'Who uses a system decides which law governs its accessibility. Title II and Section 504 govern it for students and the public, and Title I and Section 504 govern it for employees and applicants.', full: 'The 2024 Title II rule covers the web content and mobile apps a public entity provides. It does not govern employment. Under 28 CFR 35.140, Title I and the EEOC\'s regulation at 29 CFR Part 1630 govern employment by a public entity that Title I covers, and DOJ says in the rule\'s preamble that complying with subpart H does not relieve a public entity of those Title I obligations.\n\nTitle I\'s duty runs to each person. The employer makes a reasonable accommodation for a known limitation, finds it through an interactive process, and may refuse only on a showing of undue hardship. EO 1111 runs one interactive process for students and a separate one for employees and applicants.\n\nMany campus systems serve both groups. Faculty teach in the learning management system, and every employee uses the HR platforms. Before a campus can say which duty applies to a system it buys or fixes, it has to know who uses the system.'},
  {handle: 'principle:bounded-duty-burden-limits', short: 'The duty to remediate, or to provide an equally effective alternative, has limits. The institution need not take an action that would fundamentally alter a program or impose undue financial and administrative burdens, but it must still take other action that gives people with disabilities the program\'s benefits to the maximum extent possible.', full: 'The duty to remediate or to provide an alternative has limits. Under 28 CFR 35.150(a)(3), 35.164 and 35.204, a public entity need not take an action it can show would fundamentally alter a service, program or activity, or would impose undue financial and administrative burdens. The limit excuses that action and leaves the duty in place. The entity must still take any other action that avoids the alteration or burden and still gives people with disabilities its benefits and services to the maximum extent possible.\n\nThe public entity carries the burden of proof. Under 35.204, the head of the entity or a designee decides, after considering all resources available to fund and operate the program, and records the decision in a written statement of reasons. An operating unit\'s report that the work is hard does not invoke the limit.\n\nWithout this limit, the alternative-access commitment reads as absolute: every non-conformant resource would need an equally effective substitute, however large the resource. For a large enough platform, the unit that subscribes to it cannot build an equivalent substitute. The honest answer then is a documented determination with the remaining mitigation listed, rather than a demand nobody can meet or an exception nobody records.\n\nThe limit applies to one action. It leaves in place the standing duty that made the action necessary. The institution must still develop, procure, maintain and use accessible technology, and a burden determination about one remediation does not relax that requirement for the next purchase. Accommodating one person does not discharge the duty either. The duty to make the program accessible and the duty to accommodate an individual run in parallel, and meeting the second leaves the first where it was.'},
  {handle: 'principle:capability-maturity-over-binary-compliance', short: 'The CSU grades accessibility by levels of organizational capability that improve over time, not as compliant or non-compliant.', full: 'The CSU ATI policy grades each campus\'s success indicators by levels of organizational capability: Not Started, Initiated, Defined, Established, Managed and Optimized. The policy calls this a capabilities maturity strategy, and aims it at processes that deliver accessible services reliably and keep improving. Accessibility on this view is a capability the institution builds into its processes, not an endpoint it reaches once. A success indicator with a timeline must reach Established within it.\n\nThis is why the graph records status as a maturity level rather than a pass or fail flag, and why a move from one level to the next is the unit of progress.'},
  {handle: 'principle:closest-to-capacity', short: 'The party closest to the capacity to fix an asset is responsible for remediating it. Where that party cannot remediate, responsibility rises to the institution.', full: 'The rule has two steps, and only the first is intuitive. First, the party closest to the capacity to fix an interface or asset is responsible for remediating it. That is normally the unit that builds, hosts or maintains it. Second, where that party cannot remediate, responsibility rises to the institution instead of stopping there. A unit that selects a product, subscribes to it and reports its problems, but cannot modify it, is a steward without the capacity to remediate, and the duty passes upward.\n\nCapacity is separate from liability. Title II places the duty on the public entity, and Section 508 places it on the agency as a whole. Neither divides it among units inside. The institution stays liable throughout, so this rule allocates work, not blame. A second allocation runs alongside it: capacity decides who remediates, but the decision to deploy a non-conformant technology decides who pays to accommodate the people it excludes.\n\nThis is why the graph records stewardship (procures, develops, maintains, uses) separately from remediation accountability. It is also why a stewarded asset with no remediating implementation is an elevation signal to surface, not a data gap to fill.'},
  {handle: 'principle:conformance-to-an-external-technical-standard', short: 'Digital accessibility is measured against a fixed, published technical standard that the law incorporates by reference, not against an internal judgment of what is accessible enough.', full: 'The institution measures digital accessibility against a published, versioned technical standard that a third party maintains and the law incorporates by reference. The institution does not control that standard and cannot quietly relax it. The version the law names is the binding floor. The institution may adopt a later version, but a later version does not lower the floor.\n\nThree parts of the model follow. The external standard decides what the components are and what conformance to each one means. The standard stays separate from the institution\'s own assessment of where it stands against it. The model records which version governs as the standard changes. It records the standard and version on this principle\'s derives_from edges, not in this text, so a new version does not need a new principle.\n\nConformance is the test the law applies, though it does not prove that a given person can use the resource. A student can meet a barrier in a product that passes every success criterion.'},
  {handle: 'principle:continuous-sustained-remediation', short: 'Accessibility is an ongoing, multi-year process of removing barriers, not a one-time achievement.', full: 'Accessibility barriers build up over years, and removing them often takes years. The CSU ATI policy asks campuses to remove more barriers each year during that period. The commitment concerns how the work runs over time, not any particular schedule. The work is never finished, so the institution owes incremental barrier removal every year rather than a single point of compliance.\n\nThis is why campuses submit evidence on an annual cycle, why the graph tracks progress across academic years, and why the direction of an effort counts independently of its current status.'},
  {handle: 'principle:deference-earned-through-documented-process', short: 'Courts and OCR defer to an institution\'s judgment that a requirement is essential, or that a change would fundamentally alter a program, only when a record shows the responsible officials considered the individual and the alternatives.', full: 'Courts and OCR give substantial deference to a college\'s judgment that an academic requirement is essential or that a modification would fundamentally alter a program, but the deference is conditional. The Section 504 regulation at 34 CFR 104.44(a) treats an academic requirement as non-discriminatory only if the recipient can demonstrate that it is essential. The Title II regulation at 28 CFR 35.130(b)(7)(i) excuses a modification only if the public entity can demonstrate a fundamental alteration.\n\nCourts have read that burden as a process requirement. In Wynne v. Tufts University School of Medicine (1st Cir. 1991 and 1992), Tufts had to show that its relevant officials considered alternative means, their feasibility, cost and effect on the program, and reached a rationally justifiable conclusion. An assertion with no record behind it gets no deference.\n\nTwo things follow. The faculty and academic administrators responsible for a course decide whether a change would fundamentally alter it, after an individualized look at the student and the alternatives, and they keep the record of that look. A claim that an accessible version of a platform or material cannot be provided needs the same documented consideration, including whether technology has changed what is feasible.\n\nThis principle governs how a limit is established. Where the limits sit is the subject of the principle that undue burden and fundamental alteration bound the duty.'},
  {handle: 'principle:equally-effective-access', short: 'Accessible technology must give a person with a disability the same result, benefit or opportunity as others, not merely some alternative.', full: 'Accessible technology must give the same result, benefit or opportunity, not merely some alternative. The Title II and Section 504 regulations prohibit an aid, benefit or service that is not as effective as the one provided to others (28 CFR 35.130(b)(1)(iii); 34 CFR 104.4(b)(1)(iii)), and the CSU ATI policy uses the same words.\n\nEqual effectiveness is a set of independent conditions, not a scale. The CSU TAAP form tests an alternative against six. It must deliver the same information, engagement and services. It must offer the same availability. The person must be able to use it independently, without additional assistance. It must impose no disparate burden on that person. Its ease of use must be substantially equivalent. It must protect the person\'s privacy.\n\nAn alternative that fails one condition is not mostly equally effective. It falls into a lesser category with heavier obligations. That is why the form grades an alternative into one of three outcomes rather than a score: equally effective; a non-equal alternative that may still require individual accommodation; or no viable alternative, where each person\'s accommodation is arranged case by case.\n\nIndependence carries particular weight, and 28 CFR 35.160(b)(2) names it alongside privacy. An alternative a person can reach only by asking someone for help is not independent, so any request-gated route is a lesser outcome however well it is staffed.'},
  {handle: 'principle:federal-funding-reaches-every-operation', short: 'Because the CSU receives federal financial assistance, Section 504 applies to all of its operations, not only to the program the funds pay for.', full: 'Section 504 prohibits disability discrimination in any program or activity that receives federal financial assistance. The statute, at 29 U.S.C. 794(b)(2)(A), defines program or activity as all of the operations of a college, university or public system of higher education. ED\'s regulation repeats the definition at 34 CFR 104.3(k).\n\nOne federal grant therefore brings every CSU operation under Section 504, including operations the 2024 Title II rule excepts or does not reach. ED\'s regulation sets no web technical standard. Section 504 reaches technology through its bans on discrimination and its requirements for academic adjustments and auxiliary aids, not through a conformance date.'},
  {handle: 'principle:institution-wide-responsibility', short: 'The duty to provide accessible technology belongs to the institution as a whole and needs leadership across it. No single office owns it.', full: 'The duty to provide accessible technology belongs to the institution as a whole. Title II places it on the public entity, and Section 504 places it on the recipient of federal funds across all of its operations. Neither assigns it to an office inside the institution.\n\nCSU policy says the same. The ATI policy calls technology accessibility "an institution-wide responsibility that requires commitment and involvement from leadership across the enterprise." EO 1111 makes each campus president and the chancellor responsible for technology programs with adequate administrative support and resources.\n\nTwo consequences follow. No single office owns the duty, whether disability services, information technology or an accessibility team, and handing the work to one of them does not move the obligation off the institution. Because the duty reaches every program, leadership commits resources across all of them rather than treating accessibility as one unit\'s job.\n\nThis principle settles where the duty sits. How units divide and coordinate the work is the subject of the shared-responsibility principle. Who remediates a given asset is the subject of the closest-to-capacity principle.'},
  {handle: 'principle:local-adaptation-flexibility', short: 'Each campus adapts how it carries out the ATI to its own conditions, within shared systemwide goals.', full: 'The CSU fixes the goals and success indicators and leaves each campus to adapt the means. The ATI policy says each campus faces its own challenges in implementing the ATI, and gives campuses adequate flexibility to manage implementation. The Executive Sponsors Steering Committee and the ATI Priority Area Communities of Practice helped design that approach, so the people who do the work shape how it is done.\n\nThe flexibility has a limit. A campus may reshape the shared framework to fit local conditions only while it keeps the core elements, so the method varies and the substance does not. The limit cuts both ways. A standard or threshold used to judge the work is substance, and it does not vary by campus: every campus must reach Established on the success indicators that carry a timeline. The procedure for meeting and evidencing it is method, and it does vary. Each campus also chooses which other indicators to work on according to its capacity.\n\nThis is why the schema holds the shared structure and the data holds each campus\'s particulars, so one model fits every campus.'},
  {handle: 'principle:notice-as-part-of-access', short: 'A remedy the affected person cannot find is not a remedy. Telling people about known barriers and the way around them is part of providing access, not paperwork that follows it.', full: 'An alternative route works only for someone who knows about it before they need it. A plan kept on file and never announced leaves the person it was written for to meet the barrier unwarned, spend effort on it, and conclude that nothing is available. Disclosure is part of the remedy, not overhead attached to it. To the person who needs it, an alternative nobody can find is the same as no alternative.\n\nTwo obligations follow. The institution states known barriers where people meet the technology, with the alternative and a named contact for help, instead of disclosing them on request after someone has been blocked. The institution also tells the people who support and advise the affected population directly, instead of expecting them to find out.\n\nNotice has a limit. It is owed to the people who need it and to those who support them, which is not the same as publishing an inventory of institutional weaknesses to everyone.'},
  {handle: 'principle:parallel-duties-conformance-and-accommodation', short: 'Conforming to the technical standard does not discharge the duty to accommodate an individual, and accommodating an individual does not excuse content that fails the standard.', full: 'Title II now imposes two duties at the same time. The 2024 rule requires web content and mobile apps to conform to WCAG 2.1 Level AA by a fixed date, whether or not anyone has asked. The effective communication duty in 28 CFR 35.160 and Section 504\'s auxiliary aids requirement at 34 CFR 104.44(d) still apply to each person who needs a different format, including for content the rule excepts. DOJ says in the rule\'s preamble that public entities must meet all of Title II\'s existing requirements both before and after their compliance date.\n\nCSU policy gives the individual duty a procedure. EO 1111 requires the CSU to conduct an interactive process with each student with a disability to identify reasonable accommodations.\n\nA campus that conforms has reduced how often it must accommodate, but it has not removed the duty. A campus that accommodates one person has not made the content conform for the next.'},
  {handle: 'principle:prioritization-by-impact', short: 'Because resources are finite, campuses direct accessibility work at the barriers with the greatest impact rather than spreading it evenly.', full: 'Staffing, time and tools are finite in any year, so the CSU ATI policy asks campuses to select work that targets the barriers with the greatest impact. Campuses weigh impact, probability and capacity using the ATI Prioritization Framework or a comparable process.\n\nTwo corollaries follow. Effort spread evenly is effort withheld from where it would matter most. A control that withholds a resource from everyone also causes its own loss of access, so its access cost is weighed against the barriers it exists to prevent.\n\nThis is why tracked work carries a priority level. The principle connects to the minimal-impact test in 28 CFR 35.205, under which a public entity meets the Title II rule despite non-conformance whose effect is so minimal that people with disabilities can still use the content on substantially equal terms.'},
  {handle: 'principle:program-accessibility-as-proactive-duty', short: 'The institution must make its programs, services and activities accessible in advance, whether or not anyone has asked.', full: 'The institution must make its programs, services and activities readily accessible to and usable by people with disabilities (28 CFR 35.150(a); 28 CFR 35.200(a)). The duty is anticipatory and systemic. The institution builds accessibility into its digital presence before anyone needs it and for everyone who uses it, instead of producing it in response to one person\'s request. Program accessibility is a standing obligation on the interfaces and assets the institution offers. It is separate from individual accommodation, and accommodation never satisfies it.\n\nThe 2024 Title II rule makes the anticipatory duty explicit for web content and mobile apps. Outside that rule, Colker argues that the ADA still lets an entity wait for a request, so the sources ground this principle partly as a position the institution takes and not only as a reading of the statute.\n\nThe unit at which the duty is measured, and the choice of method that follows, belong to the principle that access is measured across the program. Compliance dates and the instruments that set them live on the governance nodes this principle derives from, not in this text.'},
  {handle: 'principle:program-viewed-in-its-entirety', short: 'The duty is to make each program accessible when viewed in its entirety, so the institution may choose among effective methods instead of making every facility or artifact accessible, giving priority to the most integrated setting. The 2024 Title II rule narrows that choice for web content and mobile apps.', full: 'Section 504 and Title II measure access at the level of the program. ED\'s regulation at 34 CFR 104.22 requires a recipient to operate its program so that each part, viewed in its entirety, is readily accessible. It does not require every existing facility, or every part of one, to be accessible. A recipient may use any effective method, such as moving a class to an accessible building, and need not make structural changes where other methods work. The Title II regulation at 28 CFR 35.150 uses the same test and requires the entity to give priority to methods that offer programs in the most integrated setting appropriate.\n\nThis choice of method is why an alternative format or route can satisfy the duty. The 2024 Title II rule narrows the choice for web content and mobile apps. Content must conform to WCAG 2.1 Level AA, and a conforming alternate version is allowed only where technical or legal limits make direct accessibility impossible. The program-level test still governs what falls outside the rule\'s technical scope, and it still shapes how a campus answers an individual\'s request.'},
  {handle: 'principle:proportionate-remedy', short: 'The weight of the remedy must match the severity of the barrier. Formal alternative-access planning applies to barriers that block essential functions, not to every conformance flaw.', full: 'Not every departure from a technical standard calls for the same response. The institution uses formal alternative-access planning for barriers severe enough to stop people performing essential functions, and the CSU TAAP process describes the plan as an interim measure for known barriers that affect core functionality. A conformance failure that leaves core functions usable does not trigger it. Treating it as though it did misallocates effort and adds no rigor.\n\nThe reason follows from finite capacity. A process applied to every imperfection uses up the attention severe barriers need. Its volume of documentation also hides the institution\'s actual position instead of showing it.\n\nThe distinction has a legal basis. Under 28 CFR 35.205, a public entity meets the Title II rule despite non-conformance whose effect is so minimal that people with disabilities can still obtain the same information, engage in the same interactions, complete the same transactions and receive the same services, with substantially equivalent timeliness, privacy, independence and ease of use. The entity must be able to demonstrate that. Proportionality keeps minimal-impact non-conformance apart from non-conformance that excludes people, instead of collapsing every finding into one category.'},
  {handle: 'principle:shared-responsibility-requiring-coordination', short: 'Meeting the accessibility duty takes coordinated, ongoing work by many units, and a coordinating function exists to organize it.', full: 'The institution-wide principle says where the duty sits. This one says how the duty is met. EO 1111 calls accessibility a shared responsibility that requires a coordinated, ongoing campus-wide and systemwide effort, and the CSU ATI policy repeats the sentence for information technology. No single unit produces institutional accessibility on its own, so the work is a collective action problem that needs someone coordinating it.\n\nLaw and policy name that someone. Title II requires a public entity with 50 or more employees to designate a coordinator (28 CFR 35.107(a)), and Section 504 requires one at 15 or more (34 CFR 104.7(a)). EO 1111 has each president and the chancellor appoint an ATI Executive Sponsor, who convenes a campus ATI Steering Committee. The 2024 CSU memorandum asks each campus to confirm an ATI Coordinator, separate from the Executive Sponsor, to oversee daily compliance.\n\nThe principle is the root of the coordinating role in the model. It is also the root of the distinction between line authority, which the units that build and operate technology hold, and functional authority, which the accessibility function holds while being accountable for an outcome it does not directly control. It is why working groups carry accountability for work, and why a coordinating body spans the distributed owners.'},
  {handle: 'principle:state-law-adopts-the-federal-standard', short: 'California law makes the federal Section 508 standard the accessibility requirement for state entities when they develop, procure, maintain or use technology. CSU policy applies it to the CSU.', full: 'Section 508 binds federal agencies, not states, but California imports it. Government Code 7405(a) requires state governmental entities that develop, procure, maintain or use electronic or information technology to comply with Section 508 and its regulations. Section 7405(b) requires contractors with entities subject to Government Code 11135, which names the CSU, to agree to respond to and resolve accessibility complaints about their products.\n\nThe CSU ATI policy applies Section 508 to the CSU and cites Government Code 11135 for it. The current text of 11135 adopts the Title II standard and does not mention Section 508. Section 7405 is the provision that adopts it.\n\nSection 508 is therefore the CSU\'s standard for technology it develops, procures, maintains or uses. It is separate from the Title II rule\'s WCAG 2.1 Level AA requirement for web content and mobile apps. The two overlap, because the revised Section 508 standards incorporate WCAG 2.0 Level AA, but they are different requirements with different scopes, and only the Title II rule sets a deadline.'},
  {handle: 'principle:time-bound-alternative-when-not-conformant', short: 'Where program content cannot yet meet the standard, the institution provides a documented, time-limited, equally effective alternative while it remediates, and does not treat the non-conformance as acceptable.', full: 'Where content does not meet the governing standard, the institution must provide an effective way to access it while remediation proceeds, and it keeps the exception temporary. Non-conformance is a temporary state the institution manages, with an affirmative duty to provide alternative access. It is not a resting state. The CSU carries this out through its Temporary Alternate Access Plan (TAAP), formerly the Equally Effective Access Plan. The ATI policy requires a plan for any ICT product that is not fully Section 508 compliant.\n\nThe time limit is a condition. An exception stays valid only while the party with the capacity to remediate shows progress. If a review finds no measurable progress, the exception lapses instead of renewing by default. Continuing to use the technology then becomes an explicit acceptance of residual risk, recorded by the authority that bears the risk. TAAP version 3.3 requires formal executive acknowledgment when a plan continues without vendor progress. An exception that renews every year without evidence of progress is a permanent exemption with a temporary label.\n\nAn alternative does not make the content compliant. TAAP version 3.3 says a plan does not indicate Title II compliance. The 2024 Title II rule allows a conforming alternate version only where technical or legal limits make direct accessibility impossible (28 CFR 35.202), and it provides no general interim alternative while content is remediated. The plan manages access and risk during remediation. It does not cure the non-conformance.\n\nThis principle grounds the TAAP in the model: its annual review and graded outcomes, the treatment of non-conformance as an active condition that needs a covering plan, and the elevation signal when a non-conformant asset has neither remediating work nor a covering alternative.'},
  {handle: 'principle:universal-design-over-accommodation', short: 'Designing for accessibility up front reduces the need for individual accommodations after the fact.', full: 'Accessibility built into technology when it is designed, acquired or configured serves everyone who meets it, and nobody has to identify as needing it. Accommodation works the other way. The institution arranges it for one person, after that person has met a barrier and asked. The CSU ATI policy states that implementing Universal Design principles should reduce the need for, and costs of, individual accommodations for inaccessible technology products.\n\nThe two are not equal options. Section 508 and Government Code 7405 apply the accessibility requirement when technology is developed, procured, maintained or used, before anyone asks. A program that relies on accommodation to deliver access has not reduced its obligation. It has turned a standing obligation into a recurring one, and moved the cost of access onto the people the technology excludes.\n\nDesign does not remove the need for accommodation. Some needs conflict, so a design that serves one group can fail another, and an individual response stays necessary alongside it. Studies have not shown that Universal Design for Learning improves educational outcomes, which qualifies claims about teaching but not claims about removing barriers from technology.'},
  {handle: 'principle:vendor-leverage-procurement-as-accessibility-lever', short: 'The institution advances accessibility at the point of purchase, shaping vendor products through the procurement process and partnerships with vendors.', full: 'The institution can advance accessibility at the point of purchase instead of only remediating a product after it is in use. The CSU ATI policy says the CSU seeks to improve product accessibility through partnerships and by leveraging the procurement process. In the CSU ATI procurement process, the ATI reviewer approves or denies an acquisition and sends contract requirements to the buyer: a vendor accessibility roadmap, an accessibility statement in the product, and a commitment to current Accessibility Conformance Reports. Government Code 7405 applies Section 508 when a state entity procures technology, and requires contractors to agree to resolve accessibility complaints about their products.\n\nThe lever has a cost, and the principle is accurate only when the cost is stated. A review that blocks a purchase without product-specific findings, a stated threshold or a path to approval stops working as a lever and becomes a blockade. The access lost is itself an access harm.\n\nLeverage is also uneven. It is strongest for competitive products a campus could replace, and weakest for sole-source or centrally licensed platforms already embedded across the market, where declining to buy gives up the resource without moving the vendor. The commitment is to influence vendors upstream through findings, thresholds and timelines the reviewed unit can act on, not to gatekeeping as such.'}
] AS row
MATCH (p:Principle {handle: row.handle})
SET p.description_short = row.short, p.description_full = row.full
RETURN count(p) AS updated;
